# Save transfer phases and verify resource ownership

Context: A transfer spans local Git state, remote resources, and network
operations. Interruption must not duplicate allocation or lose work.

Decision: Use durable transfer receipts, atomic phase updates, and project
locks. Require ownership evidence before cleanup. Save recovery worktrees
when local changes conflict with returned work. Save the applied phase
before cleanup so retry does not replay changes.

Consequences: Recovery data remains on disk until the user removes it.
Ambiguous Steel allocation requires an explicit computer ID. Retained
sandboxes remain listed and manageable after work returns.

Validation: Formatting, Clippy, 38 local tests, six Docker scenarios, a
live Steel round trip, and interactive attach/detach passed locally.
Steel CLI was updated to 0.5.0-preview.6.
